# B15 — the high-cardinality aggregation corpus (issue #236).
#
# Issue #236 is about a class PulsusDB used to REJECT and the reference
# serves: an aggregation that SCANS many groups and collapses to few
# output series. Before #236 the engine applied its 500 mid-scan, so the
# whole of this file was a 422; the reference applies its 500 to the
# RESULT and serves it.
#
# Dataset: 501 distinct `id` groups from one stream through `| logfmt`,
# one line per id plus three extra lines so min/max are not ties
# (id=0 -> 4 lines, id=1 -> 3, id=2 -> 2, every other id -> 1;
# 507 lines, 501 groups). `bucket = id % 5` gives a low-cardinality
# grouping label. Lines are spread 100ms apart across the window so a
# sliding range eval sees a different subset at each grid point.
#
# CAPTURE: every expected value below is a bit-exact capture from
# grafana/loki:3.7.4
# (digest sha256:87f0a067673756a3cede1bcbf0c74875f7df9b09fddb53e399d0c576f756cfcc),
# default single-binary config, captured 2026-07-30 by the PROVENANCE.md
# procedure. The 500 boundary was verified by capture at 499 / 500 / 501
# groups: everything is served at 500 and the rejections below begin at
# exactly 501.
#
# ===================================================================
# TWO CASES ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT, AND THIS IS WHY
# ===================================================================
#
# 1. `topk(3, ...)` — plan v14 AC 4 lists it as a case to capture, and
#    THE REFERENCE REJECTS IT at 501 groups (400, the same message as the
#    `eval_fail` rows below). It is BLOCKED rather than captured from a
#    narrower dataset: a row asserting provenance it does not have is
#    worse than a missing row. Measured on the pinned image at exactly
#    501 inner groups, the reference serves `sum`/`count`/`min`/`max`/
#    `avg`/`sum by (bucket)` and rejects `topk`/`bottomk`/`stddev`/
#    `stdvar`/`sort`/`sum by (id)`/the bare leaf — the split is
#    SHARDABILITY (Loki's frontend rewrites the associative aggregations
#    into per-shard sub-queries so the wide inner vector never
#    materialises; the others materialise it and trip `max_query_series`
#    on the intermediate). PulsusDB SERVES the non-shardable ones, which
#    is an over-acceptance divergence recorded in the differential
#    ledger.
#
# 2. `avg` / `stddev` / `stdvar` over these 501 differently-valued
#    members — excluded by AC 4 itself. The reference computes them with
#    an ORDER-SENSITIVE Welford recurrence over a Go map walk and does
#    not reproduce itself run to run; PulsusDB pins the member order
#    (`pin_reduction_order`). The captured `avg` here is
#    1.0119760479041917, but capturing it would pin OUR order as if it
#    were the reference's. See ledger entry #236 (b).

load
  {env="prod", service_name="wide"}
  50ms  id=0 bucket=0
  60ms  id=0 bucket=0
  70ms  id=0 bucket=0
  80ms  id=1 bucket=1
  90ms  id=1 bucket=1
  95ms  id=2 bucket=2
